WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.488
Week five.

00:00:01.538 --> 00:00:05.127
Managing the evolution of brands, with Shannon Wright from Nestlé.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:08.712
Two brands, two paths: KitKat and Allen's.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.330
KitKat stands for a break, and three truths line up.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.339
The brand believes breaks are good.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:15.189
Everyone needs a break.

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:18.116
And the bar is made to be broken.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.652
The most dangerous person in marketing is the bored brand manager,

00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:23.639
who changes what didn't need changing.

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:25.525
Consistency isn't limiting.

00:00:25.575 --> 00:00:27.132
It's a superpower.

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:28.732
For Gen Z,

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:30.797
sixty three percent say they need a break,

00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:32.862
but sixty four percent can't switch off.

00:00:32.912 --> 00:00:35.277
So the job is the emotional break.

00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:38.049
A debate about your brand is a jackpot.

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:41.855
The argument over how to eat a KitKat drew twenty seven million engagements.

00:00:41.905 --> 00:00:43.555
Fan the flames.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.885
Allen's wants to be a playground, not a museum.

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:48.481
Stretch is mapped from core,

00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:49.653
to near adjacency,

00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:50.746
to credible stretch,

00:00:50.796 --> 00:00:52.095
to the exception.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.777
The takeaway?

00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:55.987
Consistency, not sameness.

00:00:56.037 --> 00:00:57.427
Protect your assets.

00:00:57.477 --> 00:00:59.332
Use them or lose them.
